    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 161900\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1200 PM PDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty winds are diminishing, leading to calmer and cooler weather \n  into the weekend.\n\n* Precipitation chances return to the region early to middle of next \n  week, with significant uncertainty in forecast and impact details.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the next Friday.\n\n\nThe stronger, hazardous winds have subsided, with most of the areas \nseeing lingering breezy winds that will reduce into the evening. \nSaturday will offer another batch of breezy afternoon winds, but \nSunday will be on the quieter side. Temperatures will also reduce \ninto the weekend with Saturday and Sunday seeing highs roughly 5 \ndegrees below average for most areas (upper 70s and low 80s) with \nthe main exception being Death Valley which will be right around \naverage: in the low 90s.\n\nThe next work week will see breezy winds return as another through \npattern begins to approach the region, and with the northwesterly \nflow, temperatures look to remain on the cool side. As this trough \npushes closer to the region, it will tab into some moisture from the \nPacific and transport some showers into the region. Models are still \nstruggling to nail the timing for initial rain chances and there is \ngood potential for the time of arrival to be delayed, but the \ncurrent official forecast calls for spotty showers to arrive early \nTuesday. These rain chances look to become more widespread into that \nevening, with rain chances continuing to increase into Wednesday as \nthe trough centers over southern California. The orientation of the \ntrough and it's associated low pressure center at that time will \noffer a direct line of moisture transport into the area offering our \nbest chances for rain and increased rain rate. Unfortunately this is \nwhere a lot of the models begin to struggle, with some slowing the \nlow pressure's movement, and others pushing multiple lows through the \narea. Both outcomes call for rain chances to last into that weekend, \nbut greatly differ on rainfall amounts and overall impacts. The \ncurrent forecast shows lingering rain into the weekend, but nothing \nlargely impactful. Much can change in our longer term forecast, so \nbe sure to check in as it develops! \n\n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package... \nNortheast winds will continue through the afternoon, with occasional \ngusts of 25 to 30 knots persisting through sunset. Winds will fall \nbelow 12 knots overnight, with directions becoming more variable but \nstill maintaining a northerly component. Northeast breezes return \nSaturday, although speeds will be lighter than today and are \nexpected to remain below 10 knots. VFR conditions will prevail \nthrough Saturday, with mainly clear skies through tonight, followed \nby increasing mid- and high-level clouds tomorrow morning.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty winds will \ncontinue through the afternoon, with gusts generally 15 to 30 knots \nacross much of the region. Across the southern Great Basin and \nwestern Mojave Desert, wind directions will favor northeast to east \ndirections, while northerly winds are expected in the Colorado River \nValley and northern Owens Valley. Winds will diminish overnight, \nwith most areas seeing speeds fall below 10 knots. VFR conditions \nwill prevail, with FEW to SCT high clouds spreading from south to \nnorth overnight and Saturday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
